  "display_text": "RIED\n\n—\n\nS TERA\n\nShannon Rampe\n\nIt is a rather brisk spring afternoon when Jack first encounters the giant skull. He sits perched atop his coughing John Deere Tractor and squints across the barren fields. Behind him, rows of blades turn the earth, making it new again for the crop of soybeans to be planted in a few weeks. He thinks quietly to himself, carrying along a slow careful dialogue in his head. The life of a bachelor farmer is a solitary one, but one to which our hero, should we deign to call him that, has long since adjusted. Jack’s afternoon reverie is interrupted by a hideous scraping from behind him, much like the sound of a fork being dragged across a chalkboard. He mashes the brake and the clutch and yanks the trundling machine out of gear. He climbs down from the tractor and steps past the plough blades, peering at the careful furrows of earth between his dirty boots. Jack, though mostly uneducated, has always been a quick thinker and had taken a bit of interest in geology in high school. Even before he begins his examination, he is making educated Buesses as to what the source of the sound was. He knows that tens of thousands of years ago this countryside was covered in mile-high mountains of ice. When the mountains finally withdrew from their explorations of these more southern lands to their ancient arctic haunts, they left behind souvenirs brought cna he Cte ok in aa the surface, as if their rich ‘hed Wp mAEY Seemed to bob to\n\ny made them too buoyant to hide long j arth’ Thi * Ong in the earth’s crust. This had happened in years past\n\nnow Jack felt a familiar excitement set in,\n\noh. . ee bes # great love of rocks and stones, iden\n\ntifying with\n\nit silence, their strength, and their solitude.\n\nirlon i :\n\nBhwring endurance, He has unearthed ott\n\nHe respects\n\nner boulders\n\n» the largest weighing nearly three ton.\n\ns. They now\n\nsit in a row in front of his old farmhouse, suggesting eccentricity\n\nto p